<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samsu-iluna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1749–1712 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samsu-iluna was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>. Shortly after his accession he faced a major rebellion of southern cities led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rim-sin-ii">Rim-Sin II</a>, which he crushed at the cost of destroying the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a>. His reign also saw the breakaway of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sealand-dynasty">Sealand Dynasty</a> in the south, halving the empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna.jpg" alt="Samsu-iluna" width="312" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Budge, E. A. Wallis (Ernest Alfred Wallis), Sir, 1857-1934; King, L. W. (Leonard William), 1869-1919 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Record_of_the_sale_of_land_in_the_reign_of_Samsu-Iluna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>About a decade after Hammurabi's death in 1750 BCE, his son <b>Samsu-iluna</b> faced a widespread revolt across the south, led by a pretender styling himself Rim-Sin II and joined, according to Samsu-iluna's own inscriptions, by 26 rebel kings.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 19th century BCE, under Rim-Sin I, Hammurabi of Babylon conquered the city, and in the reign of <b>Samsu-iluna</b> it was abandoned.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Uruk joined the revolt against <b>Samsu-iluna of Babylon</b> and suffered decline in the later eighteenth century, yet its temples were later restored and it remained a religious and scholarly centre under the Neo-Babylonian, Achaemenid, Seleucid and Parthian empires.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The throne stayed in one Amorite family for three centuries and normally passed from father to son: Hammurabi succeeded his father Sin-muballit in 1792 BCE, and his son <b>Samsu-iluna</b> succeeded him.</blockquote><a href="/societies/old-babylonia">Social Hierarchy of Old Babylonia</a></li><li><blockquote>The Babylonian Empire under <b>Samsu-iluna</b>, Hammurabi's son, sacked Shubat-Enlil in 1728 BCE and ended the kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/apum">Apum</a></li><li><blockquote>Southern resentment surfaced later in revolts under Hammurabi's son <b>Samsu-iluna</b>, and the far south soon broke away under the Sealand dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/larsa">Larsa</a></li></ul></section>
